There is no mathematics on this page. It is a fair copy of lecture notes on ancient history, on the Greek colonies, continuing directly from folio 65 and into folio 67. The handwriting and content match the Göttingen ancient-history lecture tradition of Heeren's school. It is probably a student's or family member's notebook kept among the Gauss papers. Nothing indicates it is Gauss's own work.

Content, in order:
(1) End of section aa, the Ionian colonies on the west coast of Asia Minor. There were 12 Ionian cities, of which Phocaea, Colophon, Ephesus and Miletus became especially prosperous. Once large empires arose in Asia, the cities were subjugated. The 'spirit of freedom' kept breaking out and throwing off the yoke. Ionia was the home of the great national poet Homer, and of Thales and his school. The Dorian colonies were founded later, on the south(west) coast of Caria; the chief cities were Halicarnassus and Cnidus.
(2) Section bb, colonies around the Black Sea. All are described as Milesian foundations, founded between about 800 and 600 BC. They are numbered:
1 Byzantium (modern Constantinople), where ships coming from the Pontus paid toll;
2 Chalcedon;
3 Sinope, 4 Amisus, 5 Trapezus, further east;
6 Panticapaeum and 7 (read as Dioscurias) in the Crimea, called the main seats of the grain trade;
8 Tanais on the Don;
9 Olbia on the Dnieper.
(3) Start of section cc, colonies in southern Italy (Magna Graecia). First comes Tarentum, a Spartan colony; the sentence continues on folio 67 ('707 angelegt').

So there are no derivations to reconstruct and nothing to check mathematically.
